{{also|go on}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|uːn}}

===Etymology 1===
Shortened from {{term|gooney}}, from obsolete ''gony'' ("simpleton", [[c.]]1580), of unknown origin.  ''Gony'' was applied by sailors to the [[albatross]] and similar big, clumsy birds ([[c.]]1839). '''Goon''' first carried the meaning "stupid person" ([[c.]]1921).
* The meaning of "hired thug" ([[c.]]1938) is largely influenced by the comic strip character [[w:Alice the Goon|Alice the Goon]] from the ''[[w:Popeye|Popeye]]'' series.
* The "fool" sense was reinforced by the popular radio program, ''[[w:The Goon Show|The Goon Show]]'', starring [[w:Spike Milligan|Spike Milligan]] and [[w:Peter Sellers|Peter Sellers]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[thug]]; a usually muscular [[henchman]] with little intelligence (also known as a 'hired goon').
# A [[fool]]; someone considered [[silly]], [[stupid]], [[awkward]], or [[outlandish]].
# {{ice hockey|pejorative}} An [[enforcer]] or [[fighter]].

